# The installer must never be reachable on a running server.
#
# install/step4.php has no lock of its own and imports phpnuxbill.sql, which
# starts with DROP TABLE IF EXISTS for all 21 tables. Anyone who can reach this
# folder on a deployed server can therefore wipe the database by submitting the
# form, and step5.php would then hand them a fresh administrator account.
#
# This file is committed on purpose (see .gitignore) so that every clone and
# every git pull inherits the protection.
#
# The lock keys off install/.installed, which step5.php writes as its final
# action. That means:
#
#   before the first install   .installed is absent  -> installer is reachable
#   after the first install    .installed is present -> wizard pages redirect
#
# so a fresh clone can be installed without anyone having to edit this file, and
# the protection re-arms itself the moment the wizard finishes. There is no
# window in which a completed install is left exposed, and no manual step to
# remember after installing.
#
# IMPORTANT: this <IfFile> block is best effort only. It was measured to be a
# silent no-op on Apache 2.4 with AllowOverride All, because a bare relative
# filename is not resolved to the .htaccess directory the way <FilesMatch>
# patterns are. Do not rely on it. install/guard.php is the lock that actually
# holds, because it is plain PHP and cannot be ignored by the server config.
# The block is kept for hosts where <IfFile> does work, so that the wizard is
# stopped before PHP runs at all.
#
# The .sql files and the updater are the part that must not depend on any
# conditional, so they are denied unconditionally below.
#
# install/guard.php enforces the same rule in PHP for the wizard pages.
#
# To deliberately re-run the installer, delete install/.installed and remove this
# file, and take a database backup first.
#
# Apache 2.4 syntax. On Apache 2.2 use "Deny from all" instead.

# Never serve the schema or the upstream updater over HTTP. The wizard reads the
# .sql files straight off disk with file_get_contents(), which .htaccess does not
# affect, so denying them here costs the installer nothing. Without this, a
# deployed server publishes its full database schema to anyone who guesses the
# filename - which is a fixed, well-known path.
<FilesMatch "\.(sql|md|ini|log)$">
    <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
        Require all denied
    </IfModule>

    <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
        Order Allow,Deny
        Deny from all
    </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>

# The upstream updater overwrites local customisations and must never be
# reachable on a deployed server. See architecture.md.
<FilesMatch "^update\.php$">
    <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
        Require all denied
    </IfModule>

    <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
        Order Allow,Deny
        Deny from all
    </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>

<IfFile ".installed">
    <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
        Require all denied
    </IfModule>

    <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
        Order Allow,Deny
        Deny from all
    </IfModule>
</IfFile>
